1
00:00:01,716 --> 00:00:06,789
A delivery rider in Karachi wants to know if they can work this afternoon.

2
00:00:07,516 --> 00:00:11,968
They ask in their own words, the way they would ask a person.

3
00:00:12,318 --> 00:00:21,037
The agent does not answer from memory. It works out which hour this afternoon means, reads the published forecast, and computes the heat exposure.

4
00:00:21,816 --> 00:00:27,259
The thermometer reads thirty point five degrees, and nobody issues a heat warning about that.

5
00:00:27,609 --> 00:00:34,504
The exposure index reads twenty nine point four, and that is the one the occupational limit is set against.

6
00:00:35,416 --> 00:00:40,128
Blue is the exposure. The amber band is the limit published by NIOSH.

7
00:00:40,478 --> 00:00:46,581
It is a band and not a line, because the limit depends on how hard the work is.

8
00:00:47,316 --> 00:00:52,031
For seven of these hours the tool will not call it either way.

9
00:00:52,381 --> 00:00:59,278
Inside the band it depends on how hard this rider is really working, and a forecast cannot know that.

10
00:01:00,016 --> 00:01:05,004
Now they ask about an hour further out than the forecast it trusts goes.

11
00:01:05,816 --> 00:01:11,251
It declines, and it does not soften it. Every reading is withheld, in both languages.

12
00:01:12,026 --> 00:01:18,187
Every formula is quoted from the document it came from. The code and the sources are open.
